# Grilled Paella Mixta (Mixed Paella with Chicken and Seafood)

Source: [Serious Eats](https://www.seriouseats.com/grilled-paella-mixta-mixed-paella-with-chicken-and-seafood)

## Recipe Name: Grilled Paella Mixta (Mixed Paella with Chicken and Seafood)

## Date: 2026-09-13

## Servings: 6 to 8

## Prep Time: 5 mins

## Cook Time: 1 hr 35 mins (plus 10 min resting)

## Ingredients:
### Sofrito:
- 6 whole dried ñora peppers or 4 whole ancho chiles (1 1/2 ounces total; 50 g), optional
- 1/2 cup (120 ml) extra-virgin olive oil
- 5 medium cloves garlic, minced
- 4 medium yellow onions (1 1/2 pounds; 600 g), finely diced
- 1 large green pepper (8 ounces; 225 g), stemmed, seeded, and finely diced
- 1 large red pepper (8 ounces; 225 g), stemmed, seeded, and finely diced
- 1 medium leek (10 ounces; 285 g), white and light green parts only, washed well and finely diced
- Kosher salt

### Paella:
- 1/4 cup (60 ml) extra-virgin olive oil
- Kosher salt
- 3 whole chicken legs (1 3/4 pounds; 800 g), thighs and drumsticks split
- 6 pork tenderloin medallions (about 1 1/4 pounds; 565 g total)
- 1/4 pound (115 g) Spanish chorizo, cut into large dice (about 1/2 cup)
- 1 can (14.5 oz; 411 g) whole peeled tomatoes, crushed by hand or blended to a purée
- 1 cup (235 ml) sofrito (from above)
- 1 teaspoon sweet smoked Spanish paprika (pimentón dulce)
- Large pinch saffron
- 7 cups (1.65 L) boiling hot white chicken stock, low-sodium broth, vegetable stock, or water, plus more as needed
- 2.5 cups (17 1/2 ounces; 495 g) short-grain Spanish rice, such as Bomba or Calasparra
- 12 littleneck clams, soaked in multiple changes of cold salted water until no sand remains
- 15 mussels, beards removed
- 1/4 pound (115 g) large shelled shrimp
- Lemon wedges, for serving

## Instructions:

**Sofrito:**
1. Place dried peppers (if using) in a heatproof bowl, cover with boiling water, and weigh down to submerge. Let stand until fully softened, 30 minutes to 1 hour. (Skip to step 3 if not using peppers.)
2. Drain peppers and discard stems and seeds. Scrape the flesh from the skins with a paring knife; discard skins.
3. Heat oil over medium-high heat in a 3-quart saucepan. Add garlic, onion, green pepper, red pepper, leek, and scraped chile flesh; season lightly with salt and cook, stirring, until vegetables release their liquid and begin to brown, about 10 minutes.
4. Lower heat to medium-low and continue cooking, stirring and scraping frequently, until deep golden brown and sweet-tasting, about 45 minutes longer. Yields about 2 cups; can be refrigerated up to 5 days.

**Paella:**
1. Set up a charcoal grill on a level surface. Light a full chimney of charcoal; once covered in gray ash, spread coals evenly. Set the cooking grate and a 17-inch paella pan on top.
2. Add oil to the pan, spreading it to coat the surface, and heat until lightly smoking.
3. Season chicken and pork with salt; sear until deeply browned on both sides, about 8 minutes. Remove pork to a platter; push chicken to the outer edge of the pan.
4. Add chorizo and cook until fat starts to render. Add tomato purée, sofrito, paprika, and saffron; cook, stirring and scraping, until the sauce browns slightly, about 2 minutes.
5. Add stock (or water) and bring to a rolling boil. Season lightly with salt, then sprinkle in rice evenly, submerging all grains. Swirl gently to distribute; return to a boil.
6. Nestle clams and mussels into the liquid; cook undisturbed for 8 minutes. Add shrimp and return the pork to the pan, nestling everything into the rice.
7. If needed, raise the pan farther from the heat (using foil or bricks under the grate) to prevent scorching, especially if the grill is still very hot.
8. Continue cooking until rice is al dente and liquid is absorbed, about 8 more minutes, turning clams, mussels, shrimp, and pork once. Add more hot stock/water if the rice needs more time. Check the bottom for scorching.
9. Remove from heat and rest 10 minutes before serving with lemon wedges.

## Notes:
- Dried ñora peppers give the sofrito an earthy note; ancho chiles are a close substitute but spicier. Peppers can be omitted entirely.
- Follow doneness cues over stated times — grill heat varies a lot and will change actual timing at each step.
- Special equipment: 17-inch paella pan, long metal spatula, aluminum foil and/or bricks, charcoal grill, chimney starter.
- Make-ahead: sofrito keeps refrigerated up to 5 days; this recipe makes more sofrito than needed, so save the rest for braises, sauces, etc.

